    "body": "Volkswagen opened orders for the entry-level ID. Cross with the 37 kWh battery, starting at about $31,000 (€27,995).\nVolkswagen ID. Cross with 37 kWh battery goes on sale\nThe ID. Cross is a “true Volkswagen,” the brand’s CEO Thomas Schäfer said last month after the electric SUV entered production at its Pamplona, Spain, plant.\nAs the electric equivalent of its best-selling T-Cross, Schäfer said the ID. Cross is “modern, reliable, and above, all affordable.” It follows the ID. Polo as the first electric SUV from its new entry-level EV lineup.\nAs promised, Volkswagen opened orders for the entry-level ID. Cross Trend this week, starting at €27,995 in Germany, including VAT, or about $31,500.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nThe new variant uses a 37 kWh battery and a 114 hp (85 kW) electric motor, providing a WLTP range of up to 201 miles (324 km).\nVolkswagen opened orders for the ID. Cross with the larger 52 kWh battery in July, with a WLTP range of up to 265 miles (427 km).\nVolkswagen ID. Cross with the 37 kWh battery goes on sale in Germany (Source: Volkswagen Germany)\nIt’s based on the same MEB+ platform as the ID. Polo. The VW ID. Cross is now available with two battery packs: a 37 kWh lithium iron phosphate (LFP) pack and a 52 kWh nickel-manganese-cobalt (NMC) pack, and three variants:\nVW ID. Cross Trend: standard with 85 kW (114 hp) and 37‑kWh battery\nVW ID. Cross Life and ID. Cross Style: standard with 99 kW (132 hp) and 37‑kWh battery\nVW ID. Cross Life and ID. Cross Style: optional with 155 kW (207 hp) and 52‑kWh battery\nWith DC fast charging up to 90 kW, the smaller 37 kWh battery can charge from 10% to 80% in about 23 minutes. The 52 kWh battery takes around 24 minutes to charge from 10% to 80% with DC fast charging up to 105 kW.\nInside, the ID. Cross adopts VW’s new interior digital cockpit design. The setup includes a 10.25″ “Digital Cockpit Pro” instrument display and a 12.9″ center infotainment screen.\nVolkswagen ID. Cross concept interior (Source: Volkswagen)\nLike the ID. Polo and ID. Tiguan, the electric SUV features a new Retro Display mode that turns the instrument panel into a throwback theme reminiscent of the first-gen Golf 1.\nThe electric version is 4,161 mm long, 1,794 mm wide, and 1,588 mm high, with a 2,601 mm wheelbase, or about the size of the gas-powered Volkswagen T-Cross.\nStarting at €27,995 ($31,500), the ID. Cross sits between the T-Cross (€25,280) and Tiguan (€39,385) in price.\nVolkswagen has no plans to bring the ID. Cross to the US, but it will launch the ID. Tiguan in early 2027 as the ID.4’s replacement.\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on Twitter , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
